{
  "id": "lesson-011-white-gold-rhodium",
  "series": "lesson",
  "number": 11,
  "title": "Why white gold is white",
  "topic": "white gold and rhodium plating",
  "music": "music-lesson-011.wav",
  "hook": {
    "text": "White gold isn't really white.",
    "gold": [
      "isn't"
    ]
  },
  "end": {
    "line": "Renders made with",
    "handle": "HeyGema"
  },
  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "White gold isn't really white.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "photo",
        "src": "matthew-front-white-render.webp",
        "focus": {
          "x": 0.5,
          "y": 0.45,
          "k": 1.2
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "It's gold mixed with whiter metals, such as palladium or nickel.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "compare",
        "before": "matthew-front-white-input.webp",
        "after": "matthew-front-white-render.webp"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "On its own, that mix still looks a little yellow or gray.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "title",
        "text": "A little yellow",
        "sub": "Unplated white gold"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "So most white gold gets a thin coat of rhodium.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "photo",
        "src": "ring-1d-render.webp",
        "label": "Rhodium",
        "focus": {
          "x": 0.45,
          "y": 0.45,
          "k": 1.5
        },
        "marks": [
          {
            "x": 0.42,
            "y": 0.45,
            "text": "Rhodium coat",
            "at": 0.5
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Rhodium is a rare metal from the same family as platinum.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "title",
        "text": "Rhodium",
        "sub": "Platinum family"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "With daily wear the coat wears thin and the warm tint shows.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "photo",
        "src": "mix585-render-three-quarter.webp",
        "label": "Daily wear",
        "focus": {
          "x": 0.45,
          "y": 0.62,
          "k": 1.6
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "A jeweler can plate it again, often every year or two.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "cast",
        "setting": "workshop",
        "name": "Re-plating",
        "role": "Every year or two",
        "cast": [
          {
            "outfit": "apron",
            "hair": "short",
            "hairColor": "#3a2c22",
            "skin": "#e2b08a",
            "glasses": "loupe",
            "prop": "magnifier",
            "expression": "focused"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Platinum is white by nature, so it doesn't need the coat.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "title",
        "text": "Platinum",
        "sub": "White by nature"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "So on most white gold rings, the white you see is rhodium.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "photo",
        "src": "matthew-front-white-render.webp",
        "label": "White gold",
        "focus": {
          "x": 0.3,
          "y": 0.62,
          "k": 1.8
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "A render shows the client that finished white before it's cast.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "compare",
        "before": "ring-1d-input.webp",
        "after": "ring-1d-render.webp"
      }
    }
  ],
  "post": {
    "youtube_title": "White gold and rhodium plating in jewelry renders",
    "caption": "White gold is gold mixed with whiter metals, and on its own it looks a little yellow or gray. Most white gold rings get a thin rhodium coat, which a jeweler can renew every year or two. The renders in this video were made with HeyGema.",
